.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt XO_ENCODERS 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xo_encoders
.Nd pluggable output encoders for libxo\-based commands
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Ar command
.Fl \-libxo
.Cm encoder= Ns Ar name Ns Oo Cm + Ns Ar option Oc Ns ...
.Pp
.Ar command
.Fl \-libxo
.Cm @ Ns Ar name Ns Oo Cm \&: Ns Ar option Oc Ns ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm libxo
library contains software to generate four
.Dq built-in
formats: text, XML, JSON, and HTML.
These formats are common and useful, but there are other common and
useful formats that users will want, and including them all in the
libxo software would be difficult and cumbersome.
.Pp
To allow support for additional encodings, libxo includes a
.Dq pluggable
extension mechanism for dynamically loading new encoders.
.Nm libxo Ns -based
applications can automatically use any installed encoder.
.Pp
Use the
.Dq encoder=XXX
option to access encoders.
The following example uses the
.Dq cbor
encoder, saving the output into a file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
df --libxo encoder=cbor > df-output.cbor
.Ed
.Pp
The string
.Dq @
can be used in place of the string
.Dq encoder= :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
df --libxo @csv
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Dq @
form also accepts the built-in styles
.Pf ( Dq text ,
.Dq xml ,
.Dq json ,
and
.Dq html ) ,
so there is no need to know which styles are built into libxo and
which are loaded as encoders:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
df --libxo @json,pretty
.Ed
.Pp
The encoders described in this page are shipped with libxo.
The
.Dq fdr
and
.Dq fullpath
encoders are only present when libxo is built with filter support.
If the named encoder cannot be found or fails to start, the command
reports
.Dq error initializing encoder
and produces no output.
.Ss Encoder Options
Encoders can support specific options that can be accessed by
following the encoder name with a colon (':') or a plus sign ('+') and
one or more options, separated by the same character:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
df --libxo encoder=csv+path=filesystem+leaf=name+no-header
df --libxo encoder=csv:path=filesystem:leaf=name:no-header
.Ed
.Pp
These examples instruct libxo to load the
.Dq csv
encoder and pass the following options:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
path=filesystem
leaf=name
no-header
.Ed
.Pp
Each of these options is interpreted by the encoder, and all option
names and semantics are specific to the particular encoder.
An option the encoder does not recognize is reported as an
.Dq unknown encoder option value
and the encoder fails to start.
.Pp
The handling of command-line options can be confusing, since there are
three levels in use, but the rules are:
.Bl -bullet
.It
commas separate
.Nm libxo
options:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
--libxo json,pretty,warn
.Ed
.It
plus signs (or colons) separate encoder options:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
--libxo @csv+dos+no-header,warn
.Ed
.It
periods separate the names within an encoder option's value:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
--libxo @csv+leafs=name.used.free+dos,warn
.Ed
.El
.Pp
The examples in the following sections use a command named
.Dq list-items ,
whose XML output is:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% list-items --libxo xml,pretty
<top>
  <data>
    <item>
      <sku key="key">GRO-000-415</sku>
      <name key="key">gum</name>
      <sold>1412</sold>
      <in-stock>54</in-stock>
      <on-order>10</on-order>
    </item>
    <item>
      <sku key="key">HRD-000-212</sku>
      <name key="key">rope</name>
      <sold>85</sold>
      <in-stock>4</in-stock>
      <on-order>2</on-order>
    </item>
    <item>
      <sku key="key">HRD-000-517</sku>
      <name key="key">ladder</name>
      <sold>0</sold>
      <in-stock>2</in-stock>
      <on-order>1</on-order>
    </item>
  </data>
</top>
.Ed
.Pp
This output is a list of
.Em instances
(named
.Dq item ) ,
each containing a set of
.Em leafs
.Pf ( Dq sku ,
.Dq name ,
etc).
The
.Dq sku
and
.Dq name
leafs are
.Em keys ,
which identify each instance.
.Sh THE CBOR ENCODER
The
.Dq cbor
encoder emits the output as CBOR (Concise Binary Object
Representation, RFC 8949), a compact binary format with the same data
model as JSON.
Containers and list instances become CBOR maps, lists and leaf-lists
become CBOR arrays, and the entire output is wrapped in a single
top-level map.
Values that look like integers or floating point numbers are encoded
as CBOR numbers,
.Dq true
and
.Dq false
are encoded as CBOR booleans, and all other values are encoded as
strings.
.Pp
The output is binary and should be redirected to a file or a pipe:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% list-items --libxo @cbor > items.cbor
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Dq cbor
encoder has no options.
If the
.Dq pretty
.Nm libxo
option is given, a hex dump of the encoded data is written to the
standard output along with the data, which is useful for debugging
but makes the output unusable as CBOR.
.Sh THE CSV ENCODER
The
.Dq csv
encoder emits the output as CSV (comma separated values), a common
format that can be loaded directly into spreadsheets or similar
applications.
.Pp
A standard for CSV files is provided in RFC 4180, but since the
format predates that standard by decades, there are many minor
differences in CSV file consumers and their expectations.
The CSV encoder has a number of options to tailor output to those
expectations.
.Pp
The CSV encoder emits the leaf values of each list instance as
.Em fields
inside a CSV
.Em record ,
which is a line containing a set of comma-separated values.
A header line naming the fields comes first:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% list-items --libxo @csv
sku,name,sold,in-stock,on-order
GRO-000-415,gum,1412,54,10
HRD-000-212,rope,85,4,2
HRD-000-517,ladder,0,2,1
.Ed
.Pp
Fields containing commas, double quotes, line breaks, or leading or
trailing white space are enclosed in double quotes, and a double
quote inside a field is written as two double quotes.
.Pp
Since the CSV encoder looks for list instances, output that has no
lists produces no records.
When used with
.Xr xo 1 ,
the
.Fl \-instance
option will be needed:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% xo --libxo @csv --instance foo \e
      'The {:product} is {:status}\en' stereo "in route"
product,status
stereo,in route
.Ed
.Pp
The CSV encoder supports the following options:
.Bl -tag -width "no-header"
.It Cm path= Ns Ar path
By default, the CSV encoder will emit every list instance generated
by the application.
In some cases, this may be unacceptable, and a specific list may be
desired.
The
.Cm path
option limits the output to a specific hierarchy.
The
.Ar path
is one or more names of containers or lists, separated by slashes:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% list-items --libxo @csv:path=top/data/item
sku,name,sold,in-stock,on-order
GRO-000-415,gum,1412,54,10
HRD-000-212,rope,85,4,2
HRD-000-517,ladder,0,2,1
.Ed
.Pp
Paths are
.Dq relative ,
meaning they need not be a complete set of names to the list, so
.Dq path=item
is sufficient for the above example.
.It Cm leafs= Ns Ar leaf Ns Op . Ns Ar leaf Ns ...
The CSV format requires that all records have the same number of
fields in the same order.
In contrast, XML and JSON allow any order (though libxo forces key
leafs to appear before other leafs).
.Pp
To keep the set of fields consistent, the same set of leafs must be
selected from each list instance.
By default, the CSV encoder records the set of leafs that appear in
the first list instance it processes, and extracts only those leafs
from later instances.
If the first instance is missing a leaf that is desired by the
consumer, the
.Cm leafs
option can be used to ensure that an empty value is recorded for
instances that lack that leaf.
.Pp
The
.Cm leafs
option can also be used to exclude leafs, limiting the output to only
those leafs named.
In addition, the order of the output fields follows the order in
which the leafs are named;
.Dq leafs=one.two
and
.Dq leafs=two.one
give distinct output.
So the
.Cm leafs
option can be used to expand, limit, and order the set of fields.
.Pp
The value is one or more leaf names, separated by periods:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% list-items --libxo @csv:leafs=sku.on-order
sku,on-order
GRO-000-415,10
HRD-000-212,2
HRD-000-517,1
% list-items --libxo @csv:leafs=on-order.sku
on-order,sku
10,GRO-000-415
2,HRD-000-212
1,HRD-000-517
.Ed
.Pp
Since libxo uses terminology from YANG (RFC 7950), the data modeling
language for NETCONF (RFC 6241), which uses
.Dq leafs
as the plural form of
.Dq leaf ,
libxo follows that convention.
The spellings
.Cm leaf
and
.Cm leaves
are also accepted.
.It Cm no-header
CSV files typically begin with a line that names the fields included
in the file, in an attempt to make the contents self-defining.
There is no reliable mechanism for determining whether this header
line is present, so the consumer must make an assumption.
The CSV encoder produces the header line by default; the
.Cm no-header
option omits it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% list-items --libxo @csv:leafs=name.sold:no-header
gum,1412
rope,85
ladder,0
.Ed
.It Cm no-quotes
RFC 4180 specifies that fields containing spaces should be quoted,
but many CSV consumers do not handle quotes.
The
.Cm no-quotes
option tells the CSV encoder never to use quotes.
.It Cm dos
RFC 4180 defines the end-of-line marker as a carriage return followed
by a newline.
This
.Dq CRLF
convention dates from the distant past, but its use was anchored in
the 1980s by the DOS operating system.
The CSV encoder defaults to the standard Unix end-of-line marker, a
simple newline.
The
.Cm dos
option uses the
.Dq CRLF
convention instead.
.El
.Sh THE FDR ENCODER
The
.Dq fdr
.Pq Dq Filter Data Recorder
encoder is a diagnostic tool.
It records the field data and the open and close events of the output
in a simple line-oriented format, allowing libxo developers to debug
formatting and filtering problems without access to the program's
source code or development environment.
FDR data is written to the standard output and can be saved for
later posting as part of a bug report or feature request.
It is particularly useful for problems related to filtering.
.Pp
Each line begins with a character giving the kind of event, followed
by a name and, for fields, a value:
.Bl -tag -width "XX" -offset indent -compact
.It Li +
open a container
.It Li -
close a container
.It Li {
open a list
.It Li }
close a list
.It Li <
open a list instance
.It Li >
close a list instance
.It Li \&[
open a leaf-list
.It Li \&]
close a leaf-list
.It Li $
a key field
.It Li =
a non-key field
.It Li @
an attribute
.El
.Pp
The first line,
.Dq r ,
marks the start of the recording.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% list-items --libxo @fdr
r
+ top
+ data
{ item
< item
$ sku GRO-000-415
$ name gum
= sold 1412
= in-stock 54
= on-order 10
> item
\&...
} item
- data
- top
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Dq fdr
encoder has no options.
.Pp
Inspect FDR data for any sensitive or private information before
posting or sharing it with anyone.
.Sh THE FULLPATH ENCODER
The
.Dq fullpath
encoder writes each non-key field on a line of its own, preceded by
the full path to that field in an XPath-like notation.
The keys of each list instance appear as predicates in the path, so
every line stands alone:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% list-items --libxo @fullpath
/top/data/item[sku='GRO-000-415'][name='gum']/sold='1412'
/top/data/item[sku='GRO-000-415'][name='gum']/in-stock='54'
/top/data/item[sku='GRO-000-415'][name='gum']/on-order='10'
/top/data/item[sku='HRD-000-212'][name='rope']/sold='85'
\&...
.Ed
.Pp
This form is well suited to tools like
.Xr grep 1
and
.Xr diff 1 ,
since each value carries its complete context.
Quotes, backslashes, and control characters inside values are
escaped with a backslash, so each value stays on one line.
Fields marked for display only are not written.
If the
.Dq pretty
.Nm libxo
option is given, spaces are placed around the equal signs.
.Pp
The
.Dq fullpath
encoder supports the following options:
.Bl -tag -width "flush"
.It Cm slax
Use
.Dq ==
instead of
.Dq =
in key predicates, matching the syntax of the SLAX language:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/top/data/item[sku=='GRO-000-415'][name=='gum']/sold='1412'
.Ed
.It Cm flush
Write each line as soon as it is generated, rather than when the
output is flushed.
This is useful when watching the output of a long-running command.
.El
.Sh THE RTOON ENCODER
The
.Dq rtoon
encoder emits the output in RTOON, a compact, line-oriented,
indentation-based text format derived from TOON (Token-Oriented Object
Notation).
RTOON carries the same data as the XML and JSON output styles, with
far less punctuation, making it well suited for input to large
language models and other consumers where size matters.
.Pp
The output begins with an
.Dq @version
line.
Each container's name appears on a line of its own, with its contents
indented beneath it.
Key fields are written as
.Dq name=value
and other fields as
.Dq name value .
Each list instance begins with a dash:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% list-items --libxo @rtoon
@version 1.0.0
top
  data
    item
      - sku=GRO-000-415
        name=gum
        sold 1412
        in-stock 54
        on-order 10
      - sku=HRD-000-212
        name=rope
        sold 85
        in-stock 4
        on-order 2
      \&...
.Ed
.Pp
A string value that could be mistaken for a number, a boolean, or
other syntax is enclosed in double quotes.
Leaf-list values are joined with commas, with a trailing comma.
.Pp
A list can also be written in a
.Em dense
form, where the field names appear once, in braces, and each instance
becomes a single row of comma-separated values:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% list-items --libxo @rtoon+dense
@version 1.0.0
top
  data
    item
      {sku=,name=,sold,in-stock,on-order}
      GRO-000-415,gum,1412,54,10
      HRD-000-212,rope,85,4,2
      HRD-000-517,ladder,0,2,1
.Ed
.Pp
A list is dense when the application asks for it; otherwise, lists
are written in the indented form unless the
.Cm dense
option is given.
.Pp
The
.Dq rtoon
encoder supports the following options:
.Bl -tag -width "dense=auto"
.It Cm no-version
Omit the
.Dq @version
line.
.It Cm dense
The same as
.Cm dense=auto .
.It Cm dense=auto
Write a list in dense form when its first instance contains at least
one field and no nested containers or lists.
Other lists are written in the indented form.
If a later instance of such a list contains a nested list, that
nested list cannot be represented and is discarded.
.It Cm dense= Ns Ar N
Write every list nested
.Ar N
or more lists deep in dense form, where a top-level list has depth 1.
Containers inside these lists are flattened into the row, with
field names such as
.Dq via/interface .
Lists inside these lists cannot be represented and are discarded,
along with their contents.
The following keeps top-level lists in the indented form while
writing the lists nested beneath them densely:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
df --libxo @rtoon+dense=2
.Ed
.El
.Pp
When the
.Cm dense
option causes a list to be discarded, a single warning naming the
first such list is written to the standard error; later discards are
not reported.
Any value of
.Cm dense
other than
.Dq auto
or a whole number of 1 or more is an error, and the encoder fails to
start.
If the option is given more than once, the last one wins.
The
.Cm dense
option only adds dense lists; lists the application makes dense,
using the
.Dq dense
field modifier described in
.Xr xo_format 5 ,
are always dense.
